# Homochiral MOF-Polymer Mixed Matrix Membranes for Efficient Separation of Chiral Molecules

## abstract
Homochiral metal-organic framework (MOF) membranes have been recently reported for chiral separations. However, only a few high-quality homochiral polycrystalline MOF membranes have been fabricated due to the difficulty in crystallization of a chiral MOF layer without defects on porous substrates. Alternatively, mixed matrix membranes (MMMs), which combine potential advantages of MOFs and polymers, have been widely demonstrated for gas separation and water purification. Here we report novel homochiral MOF-polymer MMMs for efficient chiral separation. Homochirality was successfully incorporated into achiral MIL-53-NH2 nanocrystals by post-synthetic modification with amino acids, such as l-histidine (l-His) and l-glutamic acid (l-Glu). The MIL-53-NH-l-His and MIL-53-NH-l-Glu nanocrystals were then embedded into polyethersulfone (PES) matrix to form homochiral MMMs, which exhibited excellent enantioselectivity for racemic 1-phenylethanol with the highest enantiomeric excess value up to 100 %. This work, as an example, demonstrates the feasibility of fabricating diverse large-scale homochiral MOF-based MMMs for chiral separation.

## Introduction
Chirality, which describes an object that is non-superimposable on its mirror image, is a universal phenomenon in nature. Resolution of racemic mixtures of chiral molecules is of great significance in pharmaceutical, agrochemical and food additive industries, as the living organism targets of these molecules can display various biological responses to different enantiomers of chiral compounds.[1] In the last decade, extensive efforts have been made to develop homochiral microporous materials for chiral resolution due to the growing demand of enantiopure compounds.[2] Recently, an emerging class of microporous materials, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derivative membranes have drawn attention for molecular separation. MOFs are a family of porous crystalline materials built up from coordinate bonds between inorganic secondary building units (SBUs) and organic ligands.[3] Advantages of MOFs including high specific surface area, well-defined pore size and variable chemical functionalities, make them attractive for a wide range of applications such as catalysis, gas storage, chemical sensing and drug delivery.[4] Particularly, homochiral MOFs with open pores/channels in the framework, are promising candidates for chiral separation. Several synthetic approaches have been developed for fabricating homochiral MOFs, including: (i) direct synthesis method by using enantiopure ligands or by forming SBUs with chiral linkers;[5] (ii) postsynthetic modification of parent achiral MOFs with homochiral molecules;[6] and (iii) self-assembly of achiral building units into an enantiopure morphology by the influence of the chiral environment.[7] Compared to the latter two methods, the preparation of self-assembled chiral ligands has been widely investigated; however, it is a complex synthesis process and very expensive, which limits the broad practical applications of homochiral MOFs for chiral resolution.
Membrane-based separation has emerged as a promising candidate for chiral separation due to its potential advantages including low operating cost, high capacity and ease of scalability. A few MOFs and their derivative membranes for chiral separation have been reported recently. For example, Wang et al. prepared homochiral $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{L-Lac})(\mathrm{dmf})]$ (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (bdc), l-lactic acid (l-Lac), N,N-dimethylformamide (dmf)) membrane by a reactive seeding method.[8] A maximal enantiomeric excess (ee, given in $\%$ ) value of $33\%$ for separation of racemic $(R,S)$ -methyl phenyl sulfoxide was obtained. Kang et al. reported a homochiral $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{Asp})_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})$ (l-aspartic acid (l-Asp), $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (bipy)) membrane which was synthesized via an in situ growth method.[9] This membrane exhibited an ee value of $32.5\%$ for racemic $(\pm)$ -2-methyl-2,4-pentanediol ( $R$ -enantiomer in excess). Recently, our group developed a homochiral zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8) membrane by incorporating l-histidine (l-His) into the ZIF-8 frameworks, which exhibited a higher ee value up to $76\%$ for chiral separation of racemic 1-phenylethanol.[10] Although current homochiral MOF membranes have demonstrated the feasibility to achieve enantioselectivity, fabrication of high-quality homochiral MOF membranes remains a challenge and requires complex fabrication processes. Alternatively, the use of mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) that combine the advantages of a polymeric matrix and the molecular selectivity of microporous materials can provide a simpler approach to achieve enhanced permeability and selectivity. MOFs are ideal candidates as filler in the polymer matrix to form MMMs due to the presence of terminal organic ligands and versatile chemical functionalities, which ensure good interfacial compatibility.[11] Compared with polycrystalline pure MOF membranes, MOF-based MMMs have shown attractive properties in terms of easy processing, satisfactory mechanical properties and potential of scalability, which are desirable for the practical applications of membrane-based separation. Highperformance MOF-based MMMs have been widely reported for potential applications such as gas separation and nanofiltration.[12] Despite these successes, to our best knowledge, no studies of homochiral MOF-based MMMs with high enantioselective performance have been reported.
Herein, we report a new type of enantioselective MMM derived from homochiral MIL-53 nanocrystals with polyethersulfone (PES) as a polymeric matrix. The homochiral MIL-53 nanocrystals were successfully synthesized by grafting amino acid (l-His for example) into the frameworks of MIL-53- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . As-obtained MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His nanocrystals showed excellent enantioselective adsorption for $R\mathfrak{-}(+)\mathfrak{-}1.$ phenylethanol over S-( )-1-phenylethanol. Notably, the MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotL}$ -His-based MMMs exhibited an ee value up to $100\%$ for separation of racemic 1-phenylethanol, demonstrating a facilitated transport mechanism for chiral resolution. Additionally, MIL-53-NH-l-Glu-PES MMM fabricated by the same method also showed a high ee value up to $100\%$ for racemic 1-phenylethanol, which demonstrates the versatility of fabricating homochiral MOF-based MMM. These results pave a new way to explore the application of homochiral MOF-based MMMs for efficient chiral resolution.
## Results and Discussion
The homochiral MIL-53 was synthesized by post-synthetic modification of achiral MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Al) with l-His via a classic EDC/NHSS coupling reaction (Figure 1). l-His is a natural amino acid that is cheap and accessible in the market, thus it was selected to be the incorporated enantiopure molecule. Due to the flexible framework structure and reversible breathing effect of $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ , this MOF is an ideal candidate as a host to immobilize guest molecules.[13] $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ in the form of nanocrystals with an average crystal size of $375\pm110\mathrm{nm}$ was synthesized through a typical solvothermal approach.[14] Trapped ligands within MOF cavities were removed through an activation process to increase the accessibility of pores.[15] $\mathbf{L}$ -His molecules were immobilized into $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL}–53–\mathbf{NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ by an amidation reaction between carboxylic groups of amino acids and free amino groups on MOF ligands (See Note S1 in the Supporting Information). There is no apparent change in crystal morphology of MIL-53 after modification with $\mathbf{L}$ -His, which is shown in the scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of MOF crystals (Figure S1). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern (Figure 1 c) of as-prepared $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-NH}_{2{\_}{\mathrm{as}}}})$ ) is consistent with the PXRD pattern of the large pore (lp) configuration, suggesting that there are trapped ligands and solvent inside the MOF cavities.[16] After the activation process, the PXRD pattern of $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ matches with the narrow pore (np) configuration due to the adsorption of water from air, demonstrating that trapped ligands were successfully removed from the MOF pores.[16,17] Moreover, we observed that the PXRD pattern of the $\mathbf{L}$ -Hisfunctionalized MIL- ${.53.}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His) is similar to that of the MIL- $53\ –\mathrm{NH_{2-}}$ as. This indicated the incorporation of amino acids into the cavities of the $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ induced the crystalline structure transformation of the MIL-53. The successful incorporation of l-His into the $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL}–53–\mathbf{NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ framework was further confirmed by Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. As shown in Figure S4, MIL-53- NH-l-His showed an absorption peak at $1670\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which corresponded to the amide group formed between the carboxylic group of $\mathrm{~L~}$ -His and amino group on the MIL-53 framework, whereas FTIR spectroscopy of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL}–53–\mathbf{NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ did not have this absorption peak. The amount of $\mathbf{L}$ -His $(23.1\mathrm{wt}\%)$ ) incorporated into the MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was estimated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure S5 and Note S2 in the Supporting Information).
Circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy was also performed to characterize the homochirality of the MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals. Compared with unmodified MIL- $.53\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , there is an adsorption peak at $215\mathrm{nm}$ in the CD spectrum of MIL53-NH-l-His, demonstrating the homochirality of the MIL53-NH-l-His (Figure S6). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotL}$ -His is $84.7\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is much lower than the BET surface area of pristine MIL-53- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}{=}786.8\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , suggesting that most of the pore volume is occupied by the $\mathbf{L}$ -His (Figure S7). The reduction of microporosity was confirmed by positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy (PALS), in which the average pore diameter of MIL-53-NH-l-His $(5.39\pm0.08\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ is lower than that of pristine MIL-53- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\langle6.94\pm0.44\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\rangle$ (Table S1). Solid state NMR was also performed to verify the successful incorporation of $\mathbf{L}$ -His into the MIL-53 (Figure 1d). In contrast with the unmodified MIL- $53\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , additional peaks attributed to the $\mathbf{L}$ -His can be observed from the $^{13}{\bf C}{\bf N M R}$ spectra of MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His crystals along with several peak shifts, which confirmed the incorporation of the $\mathrm{~L~}$ -His into the MIL-53 framework.
Adsorption properties of MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotL}$ -His nanocrystals for racemic 1-phenylethanol were investigated prior to fabrication of MMM (illustrated in Figure 1 e). MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is an achiral MOF, which was confirmed by the result of the CD spectrum, thus it showed non-selective adsorption for racemic 1-phenylethanol (Figure S8). However, the gas chromatography (GC) result showed the MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals exhibited enantioselective adsorption for $R$ - $(+)$ -1-phenylethanol over $S\ –(-)$ -1-phenylethanol $(e e=71\%$ ) (Figure 1 f). This provided the direct evidence that the MIL53-NH-l-His had promise for performing chiral separation.
The MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMM could be readily fabricated by casting onto a smooth glass plate, followed by solvent evaporation to obtain a free-standing membrane (Figure 2a). Figure 2b shows the SEM image of surface morphology of MIL-53-NH-l-His with $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ MOF loading (MIL-53-NH-l-His_ $20\%$ ). Uniform and non-aggregated distribution of MIL-53-NH-l-His is observed on the membrane surface with no visible cracks or defects. From the crosssection image of MIL-53-NH-l-His_ $20\%$ (Figure 2c), the thickness of the membrane is approximately $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . XRD patterns of all MMMs with various MOF loading showed identical characterized peaks, confirming the integrity of MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals within the PES matrix during the membrane fabrication (Figure S9). The PALS results of pure PES membrane and MMMs with various MOF loadings are summarized in Table S1. We observed that the free volume element size of pure PES membrane $(88.3\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3})$ is close to that of MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals $(82.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ . Therefore, with the increase of MOF loadings, the change of the free volume of MMM is small due to the comparable free volume size of polymer matrix and MOF filler. Nevertheless, the PALS results confirm that there is no significant structural difference between the pure PES membrane and MMMs, suggesting minimal change to the packing of the PES matrix from the addition of the MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals.
Furthermore, the quality of the MMMs was examined by single gas permeation experiment. The MMM with $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ MOF loading showed comparable gas separation capacity with the pristine PES membrane, suggesting the high quality of those MMMs (Figure S10).
Chiral separation capacity of MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMMs was examined by using a homemade diffusion cell, in which the $0.01\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ racemic 1-phenylethanol was added to the feed chamber and pure ethanol was added to the permeate chamber. Analyte was collected from the permeate side every $2\mathrm{h}$ and analysed by enantioselective GC. Figure 2e,f and Figure S11 show GC results of permeate solution verse time for the MIL-53-NH-l-His_ $20\%$ . GC results show peak relative responses of two enantiomers, in which the peak area of the $R$ -enantiomer is always greater than that of the $S.$ - enantiomer during a permeation test. Compared with pure PES membrane and achiral MIL-53- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -PES MMM (Figure S12) which were unable to separate racemic 1-phenylethanol, MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\cdotL}$ -His_ $20\%$ membrane exhibited excellent enantioselectivity for racemic 1-phenyethanol with a permeate ee value of $100\%$ in the first two hours ( $R$ -enantiomer in excess). Such a high chiral selectivity is attributed to MIL53-NH-l-His nanocrystals which act as chiral selectors when embedded within the PES matrix (Figure 2d). Both the concentration of the $R\mathfrak{-}(+)$ -1-phenylethanol and $S\ –(-)\ –1\cdot$ - phenylethanol in the permeate solution increased with time due to the concentration-driven diffusion, while the concentration of $R\mathfrak{-}(+)$ -1-phenylethanol remained higher than that of $S\ –(-)$ -1-phenylethanol. As shown in Figure $3\mathrm{a}$ , the flux of $\scriptstyle R-(+)-$ 1-phenylethanol declines with permeation time, whereas the flux of $_{S-(-)}$ -1-phenylethanol increases with time, due to the tendency of the permeation to reach equilibrium and concentration-dependent competing interactions with chiral sites.[18]
To investigate the effect of MOF loading on the chiral separation capacity of MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His-based MMMs, we prepared MMMs with various MOF loadings from 10 to $30\mathrm{wt\%}$ . Note that all MMMs were tested under the same conditions with permeation time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . As shown in Figure $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{b}}$ , the MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His_ $10\%$ membrane exhibited a lower ee value $(91\%)$ than that of the MIL-53-NH-lHis_ $20\%$ membrane $(100\%)$ . We hypothesize this occurs because there are fewer chiral recognition sites within the membrane matrix. However, with the increase of the MOF loading from 20 to $30\mathrm{wt\%}$ , the $e e$ value of MIL-53-NH-lHis_ $30\%$ membrane declined to $71\%$ . This might be attributed to the partial aggregation of the MOF particles creating more interfacial voids (Figure S14), which leads to the formation of nonselective diffusion pathways through the membrane, resulting in the reduction of selectivity.[12c]
Various concentrations of racemic 1-phenylethanol were used to investigate the effect of feed concentration on the membrane performance. As the concentration gradient was the driving force of permeation, increase of the feed concentration is expected to increase the flux. Figure 3 c shows the change of the $e e$ and flux for MIL-53-NH-l-His_ $20\%$ membrane as a function of the concentration of feed solution. The flux of $R$ - $(+)$ -1-phenylethanol through the MIL-53-NHl-His_ $20\%$ increased from $16.28\times10^{-5}$ to $32.56\times$ $10^{-5}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with the increase of feed concentration from 0.005 to $0.04\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , which is consistent with expectation. However, the non-selective diffusion of chiral molecules was also enhanced with the increase of the concentration gradient; thus, the ee value of the permeate solution exhibited a declining trend with the increase of the feed concentration.
We further explored the chiral separation capacity of MMMs with different thicknesses. As shown in Figure 3 d, MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotL}$ -His $20\%$ with $13\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness gave rise to enhanced flux of chiral molecules $(42.02\times10^{-5}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ ) as compared to that of MIL-53-NH-l-His_ $20\%$ with $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness $(25.21\times10^{-5}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ . However, the separation efficiency of MIL-53-NH-l-His_ $20\%$ with $13\upmu\mathrm{m}$ was compromised with the ee value decreasing to $85\%$ . By increasing the thickness of the membrane to $50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , the ee value of the permeate solution $e e=100\%$ ) can be retained in comparison with the MMM with $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness, whereas the flux decreases due to the increase of dynamic resistance and barrier properties of the membrane.
To understand the enantioselective transport mechanisms in MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMMs, we performed permeation experiments on pure PES and achiral MIL- $53\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -PES membranes, and observed that both membranes exhibited non-enantioselective permeation for racemic 1-phenylethanol. As shown in Figure S12, the ratio of $R\mathrm{-}(+)$ -1-phenylethanol and $S\ –(+)\cdot$ -1-phenylethanol remained the same after they passed through the pristine PES membrane and achiral $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL}.53\mathbf{-NH}_{2}.}\end{array}$ PES membrane. Due to the achiral nature of PES matrix and MIL- $.53\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ filler, the $\scriptstyle{R-(+)-}$ 1-phenylethanol and S- $(+)$ -1-phenylethanol exhibit same interactions with the PES matrix and $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ nanocrystals, resulting in same diffusion rates of two enantiomers of 1-phenylethanol through the membrane. Therefore, the enantioselectivity of
MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMMs is attributed to the enantiospecific interaction between MIL-53-NH-l-His chiral recognition sites and chiral molecules, as evidenced by our observations that $\scriptstyle{R-(+)-}$ 1-phenylethanol is selectively adsorbed by MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals and preferentially permeated through the MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His-based MMM (Figure 1e). Therefore, the dominant transport mechanism for enantioselectivity of membranes is likely be a facilitated transport mechanism driven by the concentration gradient.[19] The $R$ -enantiomer has higher binding affinity with chiral recognition sites than the $s$ -enantiomer that shows no or less interaction with the chiral recognition sites according to the “three-point interaction model”.[20] Therefore, permeation of the $R$ -enantiomer is facilitated by passing through the MOF channels, whereas the $s$ -enantiomer undergoes non-selective diffusion through the polymer matrix with a much slower rate.[21] Note that MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMMs exhibited a declining ee value as a function of time. A similar phenomenon has been commonly observed in solid-state enantioselective membranes, in which a maximal enantioselectivity typically occurs during the initial stage of the separation, and the enantioselectivity gradually decreases with time due to the non-selective diffusion of the competing enantiomer with weaker binding affinity.[19,22]
To demonstrate the versatility of homochiral MOF-based MMMs, l-glutamic acid-functionalized MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Al) nanocrystals (MIL-53-NH-l-Glu) were also synthesized (Figure S1c). MIL-53-NH-l-Glu nanocrystals exhibited enantioselective adsorption for $R\mathrm{-}(+)$ -1-phenylethanol with ee value of $72\%$ , which is comparable to that of MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His nanocrystals. The successful incorporation of l-glutamic acid $\left(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Glu}\right)$ was confirmed by various characterizations (see Note S3 and Figures S15 and S16 in the Supporting Information). As shown in the Figure S17, GC results of the permeate solution versus time for MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotL}$ -Glu_ $20\%$ clearly demonstrated the capability of the MIL-53-NH-l-Glu_ $20\%$ membrane for chiral separation. MIL-53-NH-l-Glu_ $20\%$ membrane can achieve comparable chiral separation performance to that of the MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His_ $20\%$ . The maximal $e e$ value of $100\%$ can be obtained during the first $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ for MIL53-NH-l-Glu $20\%$ membrane with $0.01\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ racemic 1- phenylethanol as feed solution. By testing the chiral separation performance of MIL-53-NH-l-Glu-based MMMs with different MOF loadings, we observed the MIL-53-NH-lGlu_ $20\%$ exhibited the optimal chiral separation capacity, which is consistent with the MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMMs (Figure 4a and b). In addition, compared with the MIL-53- NH-l-His_ $20\%$ membrane, similar trends can be observed for MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -Glu_ $20\%$ membrane when the membrane thickness and concentration of feed solution are changed (Figure 4c and d), but the MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -Glu-PES MMMs showed higher molecular fluxes (Figures 4c and d) than MIL53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His-PES MMMs under the same test condition (Figures 3 c and d).
## Conclusion
In summary, we have prepared and characterized homochiral MIL-53-based MMMs which generate superior performance for separation of chiral molecules by the simple combination of homochiral MOF components with polymeric matrix. l-His-functionalized $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals that demonstrated enantioselective adsorption for racemic 1- phenylethanol were synthesized through post-synthetic modification of achiral MIL- ${.53.}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . MIL-53-NH-l-His nanocrystals were embedded into the PES matrix to form a selfstanding membrane via facile solution casting and solvent evaporation approach. The MIL-53-NH- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{L}$ -His_ $20\%$ membrane exhibited excellent enantioselectivity for racemic 1- phenylethanol with a highest $e e$ value of $100\%$ during the initial stage of permeation. Analysis of enantioselective adsorption and permeation selectivity of racemic 1-phenylethanol suggests a concentration gradient driven facilitated transport mechanism for MIL-53-NH-l-His-based MMMs. The versatility of this approach to fabricate enantioselective MMMs was demonstrated by the investigation of chiral separation capability of MIL-53-NH-l-Glu-based MMMs. This work provides new approaches towards designing homochiral MOF/polymer hybrid membranes for efficient chiral separation.
